WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:04.250
Wonder Woman has arrived in theaters, and
much to the disappointment of comic-conscious

00:00:04.250 --> 00:00:07.070
nerds, it went incredibly light on the Easter
Eggs.

00:00:07.070 --> 00:00:10.572
Sure, we were treated to an island full of
Amazons that seemed pretty faithful to their

00:00:10.572 --> 00:00:11.940
comic counterparts.

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:15.590
But the film didn't have a single inexplicable
time-traveling Flash cameo.

00:00:15.590 --> 00:00:17.019
What's a wonder-nerd to do?

00:00:17.019 --> 00:00:20.900
Let's roll up our sleeves and see what secret
DC references we were able to find between

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:22.609
the frames of Wonder Woman.

00:00:22.609 --> 00:00:25.359
And of course, super spoilers ahead.

00:00:25.359 --> 00:00:27.220
We all scream

00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:30.929
Because it's a comic book movie, Wonder Woman
borrowed a huge chunk of its content from,

00:00:30.929 --> 00:00:34.290
you know, comic books… just in a very general
sense.

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:38.470
However, eagle-eyed readers of DC Comics'
New 52 version of Justice League were quick

00:00:38.470 --> 00:00:41.600
to spot a story beat cribbed directly from
the comic pages.

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:45.850
In the first few pages of Justice League #3,
Princess Diana has pretty much the same conversation

00:00:45.850 --> 00:00:49.640
with an ice cream vendor as she does in the
film, though the comic presents a more modern

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:50.640
setting.

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:54.260
The story quickly diverges, however, as modern
Diana is quickly attacked by Parademons from

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:58.010
another planet, while movie Diana remains
hell-bent on hunting down Ares.

00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:02.530
The scene gets another adaptation in the animated
feature Justice League: War, further cementing

00:01:02.530 --> 00:01:06.500
the fact that despite having a pretty good
grasp on pleasure, Amazons don't have anything

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:07.500
on humans.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:10.810
They have an isolated island paradise...but
we have Ben &amp; Jerry's.

00:01:10.810 --> 00:01:12.289
Point, mortals.

00:01:12.289 --> 00:01:13.600
Four eyes

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:16.979
Wonder Woman doesn't have a very extensive
history on screen compared to some of her

00:01:16.979 --> 00:01:17.979
contemporaries.

00:01:17.979 --> 00:01:22.509
But her 1975 TV show is a thing of legend,
mostly because it was straight-up goofy fun.

00:01:22.509 --> 00:01:25.909
What kind of Amazonian magic does it take
to spin around to change your clothes?

00:01:25.909 --> 00:01:29.000
We never found out, but plenty of people got
dizzy trying.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:32.899
Notably, 1975's Wonder Woman employed the
unrealistic trope that if you wear a pair

00:01:32.899 --> 00:01:37.319
of glasses, you're completely unrecognizable
as the same human you are without your glasses.

00:01:37.319 --> 00:01:41.039
So, when the cinematic Wonder Woman pops on
a pair of specs as part of her disguise to

00:01:41.039 --> 00:01:46.289
blend in with lowly humans, it's quickly called
out by Etta Candy as being completely ridiculous.

00:01:46.289 --> 00:01:48.960
Because honestly, it's about time someone
did.

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:50.779
Official business

00:01:50.779 --> 00:01:53.730
Because Bruce Wayne is the richest man in
the world, he can afford to send armored cars

00:01:53.730 --> 00:01:56.799
anywhere he wants, even if they just contain
a single photograph.

00:01:56.799 --> 00:02:00.609
Meanwhile, the rest of the 99 percent just
has to settle for the post office losing their

00:02:00.609 --> 00:02:02.979
birthday presents in the black hole that is
New Jersey.

00:02:02.979 --> 00:02:07.130
Still, when Bruce sends around his Waynemobiles,
you'd think he'd be a little more subtle about

00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:10.690
the fact that he's the guy assembling a group
of superhumans called the Justice League…

00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:11.690
but no.

00:02:11.690 --> 00:02:14.610
When you're Wayne levels of rich, you spring
for vanity plates, and you make sure those

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:17.420
plates have the initials of your secret super-team
on them.

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:21.200
Having the letters 'JL' lead off your armored
car's license plate is real subtle, Bruce.

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:23.280
What does your other car say?

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:24.360
"IMBATMAN"?

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:25.360
God Killer

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:28.760
So, as we learn by the end of the movie, it
turns out that the magical God Killer sword

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:30.060
can't actually kill gods.

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:33.480
And just like in the afterschool specials
of yore, the power to kick some godly butt

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:35.450
was inside Diana all along.

00:02:35.450 --> 00:02:39.040
As far as DC Comics is concerned, the God
Killer sword is generally the domain of the

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:41.850
mercenary villain known as Deathstroke, the
Terminator.

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:45.260
The far-too-powerful weapon was crafted by
one god to kill another, and was given to

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:46.860
Earth's greatest assassin.

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:50.610
So while it's clear that Wonder Woman's big-screen
sword wasn't the real deal, there are rumblings

00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:52.640
of Deathstroke appearing in the DCEU.

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:56.260
Could we get a look at the real God Killer
sword in the future?

00:02:56.260 --> 00:02:57.260
Amazon prime

00:02:57.260 --> 00:03:01.330
While it's not really an Easter Egg, DC went
all out in including Princess Diana's many,

00:03:01.330 --> 00:03:03.480
many friends and relatives on Themyscira.

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:06.720
While some were named on-screen, a handful
are only mentioned in the credits.

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:10.290
Every single one of these Amazons has had
a few comic pages dedicated to them, even

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:12.990
if most of them are largely interchangeable
warrior women.

00:03:12.990 --> 00:03:16.410
But maybe we'll see a couple of them again
in Wonder Woman II?

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:17.720
It's a miracle

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:21.230
There's not a whole lot of information about
that mystery gas General Ludendorff was snorting

00:03:21.230 --> 00:03:22.350
in order to Hulk out.

00:03:22.350 --> 00:03:26.190
But DC comics has a pretty weird history of
its heroes and villains taking strange substances

00:03:26.190 --> 00:03:28.130
to perform superhuman tasks.

00:03:28.130 --> 00:03:32.640
One early example of this appeared in 1940
under the name "miraclo," a "miracle vitamin"

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:37.670
that enabled the Golden Age hero Hourman to
do heroic things for sixty action-packed minutes.

00:03:37.670 --> 00:03:41.951
While miraclo was developed for positive purposes,
it eventually fell into the wrong hands and

00:03:41.951 --> 00:03:45.510
became a substance that Batman fans will be
very familiar with: venom.

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:49.560
Whether or not Dr. Poison's power-up gas will
eventually find its way into the bubbling

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:52.290
tubes of the supervillain Bane is anyone's
guess.

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:55.319
But the foundations have been laid to break
the Batfleck.

00:03:55.319 --> 00:03:56.520
Thanks for watching!

00:03:56.520 --> 00:03:58.800
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:00.810
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

